Codex:
It sounds weird because “client-side database” sounds like copying Postgres into the browser. That is not the idea.
It is more like: the frontend already has caches, maps, arrays, Zustand/React state, etc. A local store is just making that explicit, normalized, ordered, and durable.
Instead of:
kanban cache
company explorer cache
logic gate arena cache
sidebar cache
node graph cache
you keep:
nodes
logic_gates
projects
relationships
work_items
permissions
notifications
sync_cursors
Then Kanban, Explorer, Arena, sidebar are views over that same local data.
How It Stays Small
You do not store the whole company forever.
You store only:
the current user
the current company/workspace
entities the user has permission to see
active/recent scopes
summary rows by default
details only when opened
bounded history
Example:
IndexedDB
  nodes:
    node_123 -> { id, name, type, project_id, logic_gate_id, updated_at }

  relationships:
    rel_555 -> { from_id: node_123, to_id: gate_9, type: member_of }

  kanban_cards:
    card_99 -> { work_item_id, board_id, list_id, rank }

  sync_cursors:
    project:abc -> 18422
    user:jon -> 9021
That is not huge. A few thousand or tens of thousands of rows in IndexedDB is normal. The danger is not “browser database exists.” The danger is letting it become an unbounded mirror of the entire company.
How Updates Work
Backend writes source/projections like today.
Then backend emits one ordered delta:
{
  "scope": "project:abc",
  "sequence": 18423,
  "changes": [
    {
      "op": "update",
      "model": "node",
      "id": "node_123",
      "fields": {
        "logic_gate_id": "gate_9"
      }
    },
    {
      "op": "insert",
      "model": "relationship",
      "id": "rel_555",
      "fields": {
        "from_id": "node_123",
        "to_id": "gate_9",
        "type": "member_of"
      }
    }
  ]
}
Frontend applies that to local store:
nodes[node_123].logic_gate_id = gate_9
relationships[rel_555] = ...
sync_cursors[project:abc] = 18423
Now every UI surface that depends on node_123 or gate_9 updates from local state.
What The UI Reads
Kanban does not need to ask the server, “give me board again.”
It can derive:
all work_items where logic_gate_id = gate_9
sorted by kanban rank
Company Explorer can derive:
all children where parent_id = gate_9
Logic Gate Arena can derive:
all nodes inside gate_9
Same underlying rows. Different selectors.
Cold Load
When the user opens the app:
GET /sync/bootstrap?company=A&scopes=home,active_projects
Server returns:
initial rows + latest sequence cursor
Frontend stores those rows in IndexedDB and memory.
After that:
WebSocket sends deltas
If the client misses events:
GET /sync/delta?scope=project:abc&after=18420
If the gap is too large:
refetch/bootstrap that scope
The Real Tradeoff
This is not free. It moves complexity into a real frontend data layer.
But the payoff is big:
one backend delta
one local update
many UI views react locally
instead of:
one backend change
many surface stale events
many cache invalidations
many refetches
So yes, this is the “local store thing.” It is not a second source of truth. Postgres is still truth. The browser store is a bounded, disposable, permission-scoped replica/cache that makes the live app fast.

