Codex:
Yes, company_projection_sequences is already part of this idea. It is the seed of a sync layer.
But it is not the whole sync layer by itself.
Right now, from what I saw, it mostly supports ordered projection/event delivery: “this company/scope has advanced to sequence N; clients can detect gaps and refetch.” That is useful.
A fuller sync layer would make this the main contract:
bootstrap(scope) -> rows + cursor
websocket(scope) -> ordered deltas
delta(scope, after_cursor) -> missed deltas
client apply(delta) -> local store updated
So company_projection_sequences answers: “what order did things happen in?”
A sync layer also answers:
What exact models changed?
Which scopes own this delta?
Can a client replay missed deltas?
How long do we retain deltas?
When do we send exact changes vs refetch?
How does permission revocation clear local data?
How does frontend apply the update idempotently?

So: yes, we already have an important piece. I would not throw it away. I would build on it.
On the local database point: no, we would not create dozens of frontend tables for every project/gate/node the user touches.
You would have a small number of model stores:
nodes
logic_gates
projects
relationships
work_items
kanban_cards
folders
folder_items
permissions
notifications
sync_cursors
The user can visit 50 projects in a day, but those 50 projects are just rows inside the same projects store. Their nodes are rows inside the same nodes store. Their relationships are rows inside the same relationships store.
The scaling mechanism is not “more tables.” It is indexes and scope membership.
Example:
nodes
  id
  company_id
  project_id
  logic_gate_id
  parent_id
  updated_at

sync_scope_memberships
  scope_id
  model
  entity_id

sync_cursors
  scope_id
  sequence
If the user opens project A, you load/cache rows for project:A.
If they later open project B, you load/cache rows for project:B.
Both project A and B nodes live in the same nodes store. You do not create a project_a_nodes table and project_b_nodes table.
The cleanup rule is what keeps it bounded:
Keep active scopes.
Keep recently used scopes.
Evict old/cold scopes.
Always obey permission revocation.
Refetch if evicted.
So the browser store is not “all data the user ever touched forever.” It is more like a smarter, normalized cache with cursors.
The practical MangoRTS version would probably start small:
Phase 1: keep current backend projections
Phase 2: add sync deltas for nodes/logic gates/projects/relationships/work_items
Phase 3: frontend applies those deltas to one normalized workspace store
Phase 4: Kanban/Explorer/Arena read more from that store instead of each owning separate freshness/refetch logic
The cold truth: this is not simpler. It is more infrastructure. But it directly attacks the runtime problem you care about: fewer broad refetches, fewer duplicate surface events, less backend read load, and faster UI updates after one semantic write.

