Codex:
For go-live with ~10 users: yes, current architecture should be fine. Do not detour into local database/sync-engine work right now.
For the real architecture choice: I would stay on the semantic write path, but tighten it. I would not switch to “only source-of-truth writes, then generic projection workers figure everything out” as the main architecture.
The honest reason: at runtime, the mutation command knows the most. If the user moves node_123 into logic_gate_9, the command already knows:
node changed
old parent/scope
new parent/scope
project affected
logic gate affected
permissions inheritance affected
which hot projections must be correct immediately
which realtime scopes are affected
Throwing that away and saying “write source only, let projection workers rediscover the impact later” is not automatically cheaper. It can be more expensive because workers now have to re-read state, infer intent from generic row changes, check many possible projection rules, and introduce async lag before read models are correct.
So my answer is:
Keep semantic commands.
Keep source + required hot projections in the write transaction.
Emit compact ordered event rows / affected scopes after that.
Move expensive or non-critical fanout async.
The bad version of semantic writes is:
one mutation handler manually writes 12 unrelated surfaces
each surface emits its own event row
frontend gets many stale/refetch signals
rules sprawl everywhere
The good version is:
one semantic command
one explicit mutation impact plan
source writes
required hot projection writes
one compact realtime/outbox result per affected scope
async workers only for secondary/heavy work
So the decision is not really:
complex writes vs complex projectors
It should be:
semantic command owns intent
projection code owns reusable mechanics
outbox/sync layer owns delivery
frontend receives bounded scope changes
Example:
Move node into logic gate
The command should produce something like:
source changes:
  node parent changed
  relationship created/updated
  inherited access recalculated if needed

hot projections:
  company explorer affected for old/new parent
  logic gate membership affected
  kanban/arena affected if this node appears there

delivery:
  scope project:abc changed
  scope logic_gate:gate_9 changed
  scope node:node_123 changed
  maybe permission scope changed
That is still semantic. But it is disciplined semantic, not random fanout.
Source-only projection planning is useful for secondary things:
analytics
search indexing
notification enrichment
slow rebuilds
audit/history
large derived reports
recovery/backfill
It is not what I would use as the primary path for hot collaborative UI correctness.
So, cold answer: do not abandon your current direction. Your current write-heavy semantic architecture is the better base for low-latency B2B CRUD, as long as you prevent it from becoming “every command writes every surface.” The next architectural move should be standardizing mutation impact plans and event scope emission, not replacing semantic writes with generic source-table projectors.

