Codex:
You cannot know the exact number without load tests. But you can remove the architectural uncertainty.
Cold Answer
Your current architecture is fine for go-live and early usage.
I would trust it for:
10 concurrent active users: yes
50 concurrent active users: likely yes
100-300 concurrent active users: probably, if DB/indexes/pooling are sane
1,000+ active concurrent users in one company: not without load testing and hardening
10,000+ active concurrent users in one company: no, not in current shape
millions concurrent globally: no, not as one shared architecture layer
For millions concurrent users, the current architecture can only survive if users are naturally partitioned by company/project/scope and your infrastructure scales horizontally. It cannot behave like every mutation fans out through broad company projections, broad event rows, and broad frontend refetches.
When To Move To Local Tables
Do not move now.
Move when one of these becomes true:
frontend refetches become a meaningful part of latency/cost
one mutation causes 3-10 surface invalidations/refetches
users regularly keep multiple live views open
active companies have 100+ concurrent users
large workspaces make bootstraps/snapshots noticeably heavy
WebSocket gap/refetch behavior becomes common
My practical trigger would be:
After go-live, before any customer has ~100 active concurrent users in one company.
Definitely before ~1,000 active concurrent users in one company.
Not because 100 breaks the app. Because that is when you want the local-sync layer designed before scale forces an emergency rewrite.
Mutation Architecture Decision
Stay with semantic writes.
Do not switch to pure source-of-truth writes plus generic projection planners as your primary hot path.
Your path should be:
semantic command
-> source writes
-> required hot projection writes
-> compact ordered event rows / sync scopes
-> frontend applies bounded update or bounded refetch
Later, with local tables:
semantic command
-> source writes
-> required hot projections
-> ordered model deltas
-> client local store updates
-> UI views derive locally
So the future does not replace semantic commands. It replaces broad frontend invalidation/refetch with ordered client-side model deltas.
Final Line
For go-live: current architecture is acceptable.
For serious runtime scalability: keep semantic writes, standardize mutation impact plans, reduce fanout, then introduce local client stores before you need hundreds/thousands of active users inside the same company.
For millions concurrent users: the current architecture is not enough. The scalable version is partitioned scopes + ordered deltas + local client replicas + bounded refetch fallback.

